ng the incumbent advantage", "Prediction markets price Ager near 70% to win", "Ager's large fundraising edge ($3.0M raised, $2.2M on hand) with DCCC TV support", "R+5 district lean and a favorable midterm environment partially offset each other"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean keeps this close on paper, but the open seat, Ager's dominant fundraising, active Democratic outside spending, and markets pricing him near 70% justify a modest Democratic edge beyond the tossup consensus. The lone recent poll (D-sponsored) shows a narrow Ager lead, consistent with a small-margin Lean D call.", "watch": "Any late Republican polling or independent expenditure surge for Balkcom, or a national environment shift back toward Republicans, could quickly return this to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Western North Carolina's 11th District is an open seat this cycle after Republican incumbent Chuck Edwards renominated but withdrew, leaving Republican Jennifer Balkcom to face Democrat Jamie Ager. The district leans Republican by about five points, but the midterm environment appears favorable to Democrats, and most expert raters call the race a tossup, with a couple leaning toward Ager.\n\nAger has raised about $3 million and holds a large cash advantage, while the DCCC has begun airing ads on his behalf. Prediction markets give him roughly a 70 percent chance of winning. The race sits in the Lean Democratic column, but with a modest expected margin and meaningful uncertainty given thin public polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5972, "margin": 1.39}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6249, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6236, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6239, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5922, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5614, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6011,